          To seal marble, clean it with a pH-neutral stone cleaner, let it dry for 24 hours, flood on a penetrating impregnator, wipe every trace off within the 5 to 15 minutes most labels specify, and let it cure for 24 to 72 hours. Finish, porosity and room change how many coats you need. This guide covers counters, floors, tables, resealing, etching and faux marble.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How to Seal Marble: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use a penetrating (impregnating) sealer labeled for marble, not a shiny coating. It soaks into the pores, blocks water and oil, and leaves the stone looking unchanged. The step people skip, wiping off the excess, causes most complaints about cloudy marble afterward.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Step</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to do</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Time</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1. Check</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drop water on the stone; if it darkens within about 5 minutes, seal it</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-30 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2. Clean</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wash with a pH-neutral stone cleaner; poultice out stains first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3. Dry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Let it dry fully, at least 24 hours (48 after a poultice)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 h</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4. Apply</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spread the impregnator evenly and keep it wet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-15 min dwell per label</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5. Wipe off</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Buff off all excess before it tacks; second coat if the label says</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-30 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">6. Cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keep water, food and cleaners off the surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-72 h</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The routine is the same for a counter, a vanity, a floor and a table. What changes is the number of coats, the applicator and how often you reseal. Our <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">best marble sealer guide</a> ranks the products themselves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Does My Marble Need Sealing? The Water-Drop Test
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put a coin-sized puddle of water on the marble in four or five places and watch the clock. If the stone darkens within about five minutes, it needs sealing now. If it darkens after 15 to 30 minutes, sealing is still worthwhile, especially in a kitchen. If nothing changes after half an hour, the stone or its existing seal is holding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick the spots that work hardest: beside the sink, in front of the cooktop, by the coffee maker, and the middle of a dining table. These wear through a seal first, so a counter can pass at one end and fail next to the faucet. Seal the whole surface, but give weak spots an extra pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil is the stain that matters most, so add a second check on a hidden spot such as under an overhang. Leave a few drops of cooking oil for 10 to 15 minutes, then wipe and wash with dish soap. A dark shadow that stays means you want a sealer whose label claims oil repellency, not just water repellency.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is There a Sealer to Put on Marble That Already Passes?</h3>

          <p>
            If water beads and the stone stays light after 30 minutes, another coat gains nothing; dense, sealed stone cannot absorb more, and the extra dries on top as haze. Check again in six months. Check new installations about a week after they go in, since fabricators do not always seal. Never test with lemon or vinegar: acid etches sealed and unsealed marble alike.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-product" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Product Seals Marble? Impregnating vs Topical Sealers
          </h2>

          <p>
            An impregnating sealer, also called a penetrating sealer, is what seals marble in most homes. It is a resin, often a silicone or fluoropolymer, carried in water or solvent. The carrier soaks into the pores and evaporates, leaving resin lining the pores so liquids bead instead of soaking in. Nothing stays on the surface, so the finish is not altered.
          </p>

          <p>
            A topical sealer, sold as a marble coating, floor finish or sealer and shine, forms a film on top, like floor wax or varnish. It can add gloss and take wear instead of the stone, but it changes the look, scuffs in traffic lanes and must be stripped and reapplied. Most stone professionals avoid film coatings on polished counters.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Impregnating (penetrating)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Topical (coating, finish)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Where it sits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Inside the pores</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">On the surface as a film</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Changes the look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, on most products</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, adds gloss or sheen</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stops stains</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, slows absorption</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, while the film is intact</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stops etching</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Some etch-resistant films claim to</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Upkeep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reapply when the water check fails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip and recoat when worn</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water-Based or Solvent-Based Impregnator?</h3>

          <p>
            Both work on marble. Solvent-based impregnators often penetrate dense polished stone well, but they smell strongly and need serious ventilation. Water-based impregnators, such as the StoneTech BulletProof we rank for bathrooms, are low-odor, which makes them the usual pick as the best water based sealer for marble flooring in a closed room.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the label, look for marble or calcite stone listed as approved, a stated oil repellency, and no change in appearance. Enhancers darken stone permanently and are a separate category. Silane and siloxane water repellents are made for concrete and brick; our <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs siloxane guide</a> explains them, but indoor marble wants a stone impregnator rated for it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="countertops" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Do You Seal Marble Countertops?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear the counter and wash it with a pH-neutral stone cleaner or a few drops of dish soap in warm water. Rinse, dry, and look for stains with a flashlight held low; anything you see now gets sealed in, so poultice it out first. Then leave the stone alone for at least 24 hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mask wood cabinets, painted walls and the sink rim with painter's tape, since some solvent sealers mark finishes. Spread sealer over two to four square feet at a time with a lint-free cloth or foam applicator. Keep it wet for the label dwell time, typically 5 to 15 minutes, adding more wherever it soaks in fast, usually near the sink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before it tacks, wipe off all excess with a dry cloth and buff with a second until the stone looks exactly as before; polished marble shows residue more than any other stone. Apply a second coat only if the label calls for it, within its window. Keep cups, food and cleaners off for the full cure, usually 24 to 72 hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Is the Best Marble Countertop Sealer?</h3>

          <p>
            For a kitchen, choose an impregnator with strong oil repellency that leaves the finish alone. In our <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">marble sealer rankings</a>, Tenax Proseal is the overall pick and Dry-Treat Stain-Proof Plus the premium one. A vanity sees cosmetics rather than oil, so a low-odor water-based product suits a small bathroom. For a granite perimeter with a marble island, our <a href="/best-granite-sealer/">granite sealer guide</a> covers products labeled for both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="floors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Do You Seal a Marble Floor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sweep and dust-mop first, because grit dragged under an applicator scratches marble. Mop with a pH-neutral stone cleaner, rinse, and let the floor dry for at least 24 hours with good airflow. Plan to finish at a doorway, not in a corner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply with a microfiber or lambswool applicator, a low-pressure pump sprayer or a paint pad on a pole, in sections of 20 to 30 square feet. Keep the stone wet through the dwell time, work it into the joints, then buff off excess with clean towels. Honed and tumbled tile usually needs two coats, polished often one. Grout soaks up more than tile; our <a href="/best-grout-sealer/">grout sealer guide</a> covers products that seal both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep shoes, pets and furniture off for the full cure on the label, then mop only with a neutral cleaner. Walk-off mats at exterior doors do more for a marble floor than any extra coat, because tracked-in sand causes most wear.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is There a Sealer and Shine for a Marble Floor Finish?</h3>

          <p>
            Seal-and-shine products and marble floor finishes are topical coatings. The gloss sits in a film that scuffs and must be stripped and reapplied. The lasting way to bring back shine on a dull polished floor is professional honing and polishing of the stone itself, followed by an impregnator.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Are There Anti-Scratch Applications for Marble Floors?</h3>

          <p>
            No impregnator makes marble harder. Scratch protection comes from felt pads under chairs, entry mats and daily dust-mopping, or from sacrificial topical films that commercial floors recoat on a schedule. At home that upkeep is rarely worth it; fine scratches are polished out by a pro every few years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tables" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Is the Best Sealer for a Carrara Marble Table?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carrara is a light, fairly porous white marble that shows stains and etches, so a table needs the same oil-repelling impregnator as a kitchen counter. Choose one the label says does not change appearance, since any yellowing or darkening is obvious on white stone, and skip enhancers unless you have tried one on the underside first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tables are easy to seal in a ventilated garage. Clean the top, let it dry a day, and seal the top, the edges and the underside near the edges, since spills run off and wick back in. Keep it wet for the dwell time, then buff thoroughly: two thin coats on honed tops, one or two on polished.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many vintage and imported tables carry a factory lacquer or wax instead. If the surface feels slightly plastic or scratches look white rather than dull, it is probably a coating, and impregnator cannot soak through it. Maintain the coating, or have the top stripped before sealing. Either way, coasters and placemats matter more than the product, because sealer will not stop wine or lemon rings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="etching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Does Marble Sealer Stop Acid Stains and Etching?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. Sealer stops staining, not etching. Marble is mostly calcite, and acids such as lemon juice, vinegar, wine, tomato sauce, soda and many cleaners dissolve a microscopic layer of it. The dull mark that results is surface damage, not something that soaked in, and impregnators inside the pores cannot shield the surface from it.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why a properly sealed counter still shows a dull ring where orange juice sat overnight. Owners often conclude the sealer failed and reapply it, but it did its job: no orange color went into the stone. The etch is a separate problem with separate fixes.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE KEY TRUTH</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sealer blocks color, not acid</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Impregnating sealers keep liquids from soaking in and staining. They do not stop acids from dulling marble. Manage etching with coasters, quick wipe-ups, a honed finish, polishing powder or an etch-resistant topical film.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is There a Protective Product for Marble That Stops Acid Stains?</h3>

          <p>
            Only topical products claim to: thin clear protective films applied over the stone, and some specialty coatings. Because acid never reaches the calcite, they can prevent etch marks, and makers say the better films are nearly invisible. The trade-offs are a slightly different feel, visible edges or seams, vulnerability to knives, and usually professional installation. They make the most sense on a white marble island or a dining table that sees daily wine and citrus.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Else Helps With Etching?</h3>

          <p>
            A honed finish hides etches that would glare on polished stone. Coasters, trivets, cutting boards and trays stop most acid spills before they start. For light etches on polished marble, a marble polishing powder rubbed in with a damp cloth can restore the shine. Polishing removes sealer with the damaged layer, so polish first and seal afterward.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reseal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Often Should You Reseal Marble?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Reseal when the water-drop test fails. Run it every six to twelve months; when water darkens the stone within about five minutes, it is time. As a rough guide, busy kitchen counters need it every one to two years, sometimes yearly, while vanities, floors and gently used tables often go two to three years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Harsh or acidic cleaners, honed and tumbled finishes, sun on exterior stone and constant water at the sink all shorten the interval. Makers generally rate premium fluoropolymer impregnators for longer service life than budget silicone products, which offsets part of their price on a busy counter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resealing is simpler than the first job: clean, dry, one coat, thorough wipe-off. You do not strip an impregnator first, because new product refills pores that have opened. If only the sink area fails, reseal that zone. Write the date inside a cabinet door so you neither over-seal into haze nor forget until a stain appears.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faux-marble" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Do You Seal Faux or Cultured Marble?
          </h2>

          <p>
            You do not seal it like stone. Cultured marble, used for one-piece vanity tops and shower surrounds, is cast from crushed stone and polyester resin under a clear gelcoat. The gelcoat is not porous, so stone impregnator just dries as streaks. Treat it like a boat's gelcoat: non-abrasive cleaners, and a gelcoat or fiberglass polish or wax for shine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Is the Best Sealer for Fake Marble?</h3>

          <p>
            It depends on the material. Cultured marble and solid surface need no sealer, only non-abrasive polish. Marble-look porcelain or ceramic tile is sealed by its glaze, so only the grout needs sealer. A painted faux-marble finish is protected with a clear topcoat, usually a non-yellowing water-based polyurethane so white veining stays white.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is There Any Way to Touch Up Faux Marble Around Faucets?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. Dull gelcoat around a faucet is usually hard-water scale or wear; owners commonly report that a non-abrasive cleaner, then a fine rubbing compound and gelcoat wax, brings back much of the shine. Fill small chips with a tinted gelcoat or cultured-marble repair kit, sand flush and polish per the kit. Recaulk the faucet base with silicone. Cracks through the top mean a refinisher or replacement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Is Marble Sealer Safe to Use Indoors, and When Should You Call a Pro?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Makers generally state their marble sealers are food-safe once fully cured, but application needs care. Solvent-based impregnators give off flammable vapors that cause headaches in a small kitchen or bathroom. Open windows, run an exhaust fan, keep pilot lights and cooktops off, and read the label and safety data sheet first. Water-based products cut the odor and suit windowless bathrooms, but still need gloves and airflow.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ventilate, no flames, and handle rags safely</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Use solvent sealers only with cross-ventilation and no open flame or pilot light nearby. Wear nitrile gloves and safety glasses. Dry used rags flat outdoors or dispose of them as the label directs. Keep children and pets away until the sealer has cured.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Call a stone professional when the marble needs more than sealing: etches that feel rough, scratched floors, an oil stain several poultices have not lifted, or old sealer haze you cannot remove. Professional sealing typically runs about $150 to $400 for a kitchen, and etch removal or polishing visits start around $200. For routine resealing, doing it yourself gives the same result, because the chemistry is the same.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes With Sealing Marble
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting sealer to stop etching</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Owners who think a sealed counter resists lemon juice reseal again and again when dull rings appear. Sealer only blocks stains. Manage etching with coasters, quick wipe-ups and polishing powder, or choose a honed finish or an etch-resistant film.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the sealer dry on the surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Excess impregnator left past the dwell time dries into a cloudy haze that shows badly on polished marble. Buff each section before it tacks. If haze appears, rub a little fresh sealer on the spot to re-dissolve it and buff dry at once.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over a stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer locks in whatever is already in the stone and makes a later poultice far less effective. Look for stains in low raking light, poultice them out, let the marble dry for 48 hours, and only then seal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing damp stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water in the pores keeps sealer from penetrating, so it sits on top and wears off quickly. Give marble at least 24 hours to dry after cleaning, and longer after a poultice or in humid weather, before the first coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using stone sealer on cultured marble</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cultured marble has a non-porous gelcoat, so stone impregnator cannot soak in and leaves streaks. Clean it with non-abrasive products, polish it with gelcoat or fiberglass wax, and fix chips with a matching repair kit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a sealer to put on marble that keeps it looking natural?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A penetrating impregnator labeled for marble soaks into the pores and leaves no film, so the stone looks and feels the same once cured. Look for wording such as no change in appearance, avoid products marked as enhancers, and try any product on a hidden spot first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What product seals marble best for a kitchen?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An impregnator with strong oil repellency, because cooking oil leaves the hardest stains to remove. Our marble sealer rankings list Tenax Proseal as the overall pick and Dry-Treat Stain-Proof Plus as the premium choice for kitchens with heavy cooking, both chosen for oil repellency on calcite stone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after sealing marble can I use it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most labels allow light use within a few hours but call for 24 to 72 hours before water, cleaners or food sit on the surface. Floors usually take socks after a few hours and shoes and furniture after the full cure. Follow your product's label, since formulas differ.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of sealer does marble need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Polished marble usually takes one coat, sometimes two near the sink. Honed marble generally needs two, and tumbled marble two to three. Apply a second coat within the window the label gives, and stop once the stone no longer absorbs; extra product only dries on top as haze.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use granite sealer on marble?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Many impregnators are labeled for granite, marble, limestone and travertine together. Check that marble or calcite stone is listed, since a few granite products target only dense silicate stone. A spray granite cleaner-and-sealer is fine for upkeep but does not replace a full impregnator coat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing marble make it shiny?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Impregnators add no gloss; the shine on polished marble comes from grinding and polishing the stone. For more shine on a dull floor, the lasting fix is professional polishing. Topical sealer-and-shine products add gloss through a film, but that film scuffs and must be stripped and recoated.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should outdoor marble be sealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, with a breathable impregnator rated for exterior use, which helps against dirt and staining. It will not stop acid rain from slowly etching the surface, so outdoor marble develops a soft patina. Avoid film-forming coatings outside, since they trap moisture and can peel or cloud.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

