        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sealing marble is an afternoon job: check it with water, clean it, let it dry, flood on a penetrating impregnator, wipe off every trace, and let it cure for a day or more. Repeat every one to three years, depending on use, and wine, coffee and oil will wipe away instead of leaving color behind.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Do not ask sealer to stop etching. Handle that with coasters, quick cleanup, a honed finish, polishing powder or an etch-resistant film, and give cultured marble gelcoat care, not stone sealer. For the products themselves, start with our marble sealer picks.
          </p>
